## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend topological ordering with DP to compute the longest-path in a weighted DAG and count the number of critical paths, including multiple sources and modulo arithmetic.

## Key Concepts
- Longest-path in DAG with node durations and edge setup weights
- Topological sort (Kahn) to process in dependency order
- DP for counting number of longest paths with modulo

## Code Example
```javascript
function countCriticalPaths(N, edges, dur) {
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  const incoming = Array.from({length: N}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(N).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) {
    incoming[v].push([u, w]);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  const EFT = dur.slice();
  const count = Array(N).fill(0);
  const q = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) {
      count[i] = 1;
      q.push(i);
    }
  }

  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    for (const [v, w] of incoming[u]) {
      const newEFT = EFT[u] + w + dur[v];
      if (newEFT > EFT[v]) {
        EFT[v] = newEFT;
        count[v] = count[u];
      } else if (newEFT === EFT[v]) {
        count[v] = (count[v] + count[u]) % MOD;
      }
      indeg[u]--;
      if (indeg[u] === 0) q.push(u);
    }
  }

  let maxEFT = 0, totalCritical = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    if (EFT[i] > maxEFT) {
      maxEFT = EFT[i];
      totalCritical = count[i];
    } else if (EFT[i] === maxEFT) {
      totalCritical = (totalCritical + count[i]) % MOD;
    }
  }
  return { EFT, maxEFT, criticalPaths: totalCritical };
}
```

## Complexity
- Time: O(N + M) for topological sort and DP
- Space: O(N + M) for adjacency and arrays

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates mastery of several advanced graph techniques: DAG dynamic programming, multi-criteria optimization, path reconstruction, and cycle handling. It tests the ability to reason about edge attributes with different behaviors (skip-able vs mandatory latency) and implement lexicographic optimization where hops are prioritized over latency.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sorting for efficient DAG processing
- Lexicographic multi-objective optimization (hops first, latency second)
- Predecessor-based path reconstruction
- Cycle detection using DFS and graph condensation
- Edge attribute handling with conditional weight computation

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestPathDAG(graph, s, t) {
  // Assumes graph is a DAG. Each edge has: latency w, skip boolean
  const order = topologicalSort(graph);
  const best = new Map();
  const pred = new Map();

  best.set(s, [0, 0]); // [hops, latency]

  for (const u of order) {
    if (!best.has(u)) continue;
    const [hops, latency] = best.get(u);

    for (const {to: v, latency: w, skip} of graph.edgesFrom(u)) {
      const cand = [hops + 1, latency + (skip ? 0 : w)];
      const current = best.get(v);
      if (!current || lexicographicallySmaller(cand, current)) {
        best.set(v, cand);
        pred.set(v, u);
      }
    }
  }

  return reconstructPath(pred, s, t);
}

function detectCycles(graph) {
  const visited = new Set();
  const recStack = new Set();

  function dfs(u) {
    if (recStack.has(u)) return true; // Cycle detected
    if (visited.has(u)) return false;

    visited.add(u);
    recStack.add(u);

    for (const {to: v} of graph.edgesFrom(u)) {
      if (dfs(v)) return true;
    }

    recStack.delete(u);
    return false;
  }

  return Array.from(graph.nodes()).some(dfs);
}
```

## Adaptation for Cyclic Graphs
If cycles are present, first detect them using DFS with a recursion stack. For graphs with cycles, apply strongly connected components (SCC) decomposition to create a condensation DAG, then solve on the condensed graph. Alternatively, use Dijkstra's algorithm modified for lexicographic optimization where the edge weight is the tuple (1, w or 0 based on skip).

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical sorting algorithm design that balances cache efficiency with worst-case performance guarantees. Hybrid approaches like this are commonly used in real-world systems where both average-case speed and theoretical bounds matter.

## Key Concepts
- **Hybrid sorting**: Combines partitioning (QuickSort) with stable merging (MergeSort)
- **Introsort-like depth limiting**: Prevents pathological cases
- **In-place stable merging**: Achieved through array rotations
- **Cache-friendly recursive structure**: Larger partitions processed first
- **Complexity**: Average/worst case O(n log n), additional space O(log n) for recursion stack

## Code Implementation
```javascript
function hybridSort(arr, left = 0, right = arr.length - 1, depth = 0) {
  const n = right - left + 1;
  const threshold = 32;
  const maxDepth = 2 * Math.floor(Math.log2(n));

  if (n <= 1) return;

  if (n <= threshold || depth >= maxDepth) {
    // Bottom-up in-place stable merge sort
    mergeSortInPlace(arr, left, right);
    return;
  }

  // QuickSort with median-of-three pivot
  const pivotIndex = medianOfThree(arr, left, right);
  const partitionIndex = partition(arr, left, right, pivotIndex);

  // Recurse on larger partition first to bound stack depth
  const leftSize = partitionIndex - left;
  const rightSize = right - partitionIndex;

  if (leftSize > rightSize) {
    hybridSort(arr, left, partitionIndex - 1, depth + 1);
    hybridSort(arr, partitionIndex + 1, right, depth + 1);
  } else {
    hybridSort(arr, partitionIndex + 1, right, depth + 1);
    hybridSort(arr, left, partitionIndex - 1, depth + 1);
  }
}
```

